#
# A statement is as Spark wrote it but where a rewrite cannot change what it
# means, which its record names: a temporary view of literals as a table of
# their types holding one row of them; a timestamp's text cast to DATE as the
# DATE literal of its date (Spark's cast drops the time; this engine reads only
# an ISO date as a DATE, and a test of that cast is no test of its subject). A
# column is the engine's type of the name Spark's schema gives it. A TIMESTAMP
# Spark prints in the suite's zone is the instant of that local time in UTC,
# the engine's zone, which keeps every value here, since no record moves a time
# between zones. A result is compared as rowsort unless its query ends in ORDER
# BY, as Spark sorts every result its plan does not. An error under ANSI is a
# statement error, Spark's error class in its comment and after ---- the
# engine's own words for the same reason.
